The prompt is not the brand guardrail.

I built an n8n and Claude workflow to turn one finished source into a LinkedIn post, a five-slide carousel and a 30-second video script.

## Generation is only the first pass

Code checks the output afterward. The LinkedIn draft must stay between 90 and 230 words and end on a statement. The carousel must contain exactly five slides.

A prohibited-term list checks the language.

## Failed work enters a rewrite queue

Anything failing the gate routes to Flag for Rewrite instead of Send Drafts.

The workflow keeps the rejected draft for inspection. A guardrail should flag work, not erase it.

## An adversarial source tested the gate

I fed the workflow deliberately off-voice marketing copy. Two prohibited phrases survived the model. The LinkedIn draft also came back at 57 words.

The code caught all three failures.

## The measured result covers all three formats

Two live runs averaged 7.08 seconds. The clean source passed. The adversarial source failed for the reasons the gate was built to catch.

A prompt expresses taste. A post-generation check enforces the publishing boundary.

Built by handlit.
